A NEW module `cards/notify.py` and its NEW test `tests/test_notify.py`, both returned in full. Stdlib only, no import from `flows` or `processors`.

WHY. A run that finishes at 03:40 tells nobody. The operator learns the outcome by coming back to the terminal, which at night means the morning. One line, pushed through whatever channel the operator already has, is the whole feature -- and the line is worthless without NUMBERS in it, because an operator who has to go and look anyway has been told nothing.

DEFINE exactly two entry points.

`build_run_message(report) -> str` -- ONE line, no newline inside it, built from an archived run report: a `cards.store.RunReport`, the object whose `to_dict()` writes the `report.json` you have in your context. Import it for the fixture; the function itself only reads attributes. The line names, all of them:
  - the deck id (`report.deck_id`);
  - the branch (`report.branch`) -- and when the run owned none (`None`) the line says `no branch`;
  - how many cards were written, failed and skipped: `report.counts` is that tally, a dict keyed `written` / `failed` / `skipped`. Write them as `<N> written`, `<N> failed`, `<N> skipped`, in that order and with the number before the word, because that is what a reader scans for;
  - how many generations the run had: `len(report.generations)`;
  - how many MINUTES the run took, named with `min`.
The minutes come from the report alone, and nothing else is available: a deck id begins `YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS-` -- the moment the run started -- and `completed_at` is an ISO-8601 local timestamp of the moment it finished. The whole minutes between the two are the number. A deck id or a `completed_at` that will not parse costs the minutes, not the message: fall back to a readable `?` and still return the line.

`send_notification(message, command=None) -> bool` -- hand `message` to a shell command on its STDIN. `command` defaults to the environment variable `MORPH_NOTIFY_CMD`, read at call time. No command configured (unset, or empty) is NOT an error: nothing is sent, `False` comes back, nothing is raised and nothing is printed -- an operator who never set the variable must not have their run report a failure. A command that runs and exits 0 returns `True`. A command that fails, cannot be started, or times out returns `False` and does NOT raise: a notification is the last thing a run does, and it must never be able to fail the run. Give the subprocess a short timeout for the same reason.

THE TESTS -- `unittest`, a FAKE transport only: a `command` that is a local shell one-liner writing its stdin into a file under `tempfile.TemporaryDirectory`, never anything that leaves this machine. Cover: the message carries every number named above (assert on the numbers and the `<N> written` form, not on the surrounding wording); the minutes computed from a real deck id and a real `completed_at`; a `None` branch rendered as `no branch`; no `MORPH_NOTIFY_CMD` being a silent `False`; a failing command being `False` and not an exception; the command really receiving the message on its stdin.

THE FIXTURE IS A COPY OF A REAL ARTIFACT, not an invention: build the report with `RunReport.from_dict(<the dict from the report.json in your context>)`, pasted as a literal into the test so the test depends on no file. Do not construct `RunReport` field by field.

